<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Görres</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Johann Joseph von Görres</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Görres was a German writer and journalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. As a young radical he backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and the plan for a Cisrhenian Republic on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1797, but he became disillusioned with French rule. His paper Rheinischer Merkur opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> from 1814, and in later life, as a professor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, he became a leading voice of German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-gorres.jpg" alt="Joseph Görres" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Anton Nikolaus Settegast · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Joseph_G%C3%B6rres.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
